        "content": "<h3><strong>Quick update + Positioning Clarification</strong></h3>\n<p>A few questions have come up about how LeanCert fits into the ecosystem, so here is the specific mental model:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Certification is the Product</strong></p>\n<p>LeanCert is not primarly about doing numerics in Lean for the sake of calculation. It uses numerical methods (Interval Arithmetic, Taylor Models, Affine forms) strictly as <strong>scaffolding</strong> to build a formal proof.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SciLean:</strong> \"Simulate this pendulum.\" (Goal: Computation/Speed)</li>\n<li><strong>LeanCert:</strong> \"Prove this pendulum never swings past 45°.\" (Goal: Theorem)</li>\n<li><strong>LeanCert and CoqInterval</strong>: Both certify inequalities using interval arithmetic. LeanCert additionally supports optimization and root finding as first-class existential proof goals, and can <strong>automatically compute and instantiate the witnessing values</strong> (such as bounds or roots) inside the proof process, allowing users to directly prove theorems of the form <code>∃ m, ∀ x ∈ I, f(x) ≥ m</code> or <code>∃ x ∈ I, f(x) = 0</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Oracle-Guided Formalization</strong></p>\n<p>LeanCert helps you <em>find</em> what is true and then proves it. Formalizing bounds is often high-friction because you need to propose a concrete constant before you can verify it, and in practice this frequently leads to trial-and-error when the first bound is too tight for automation (e.g. trying 2.71, then 2.72, etc.).</p>\n<p>LeanCert splits this into two phases: <strong>Discovery and Verification</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Phase 1: Explore</strong></p>\n<p>Use #find_min, #bounds, or the Python SDK to explore numerically and propose candidate bounds.<br>\n<strong>Output:</strong> A candidate bound (e.g. “the maximum is ≈ 2.718”)</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 2: Certify</strong></p>\n<p>Verify the proposed bound using a single tactic (certify_bound, interval_roots, etc.).<br>\n<strong>Output:</strong> A lean kernel-checked theorem: ∀ x ∈ I, f(x) ≤ 2.718</p>\n<p><strong>3. The Safety Guarantee</strong></p>\n<p>Because of the <a href=\"https://docs.leancert.io/architecture/golden-theorems/\">Golden Theorem</a> architecture, the exploration phase cannot produce false theorems. If Python or the heuristics are wrong, the tactic simply fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Discovery proposes, Lean verifies.</strong><br>\nThe kernel remains the only arbiter of correctness.</p>",
